  "anchors": [
    {
      "id": "a_cold_open",
      "speakerId": "narrator",
      "trigger": "cold_open",
      "text": "Loon Lake, Minnesota. Saturday night, and Grandma June is seventy-five. The walleye is fried, the cake is cut, and at nine o'clock the whole family walked down to the dock for sparklers and Dale's fireworks. When June came back up, Old Goldie, the gold-leaf lure her father carved in 1936, was gone from its locked glass case. Four people had the chance. June would like it back before the coffee gets cold."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_lorraine_crack",
      "speakerId": "lorraine",
      "trigger": "crack",
      "lieId": "l_lorraine",
      "text": "Oh, fiddlesticks. Yes, dear, I slipped back up to the cabin at twenty past nine. My shoulders were cold and I wanted my shawl. I was alone in the great room for one minute, and a lady is allowed one minute."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_dale_crack",
      "speakerId": "dale",
      "trigger": "crack",
      "lieId": "l_dale",
      "text": "Okay. Okay. Here's the deal. At twenty past nine I ducked into the boathouse and spent twenty minutes on the phone with a guy in Duluth who wants my bass boat. The dealership's had a rough summer, chief. I'm selling my boat, not Mom's lure."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_confession",
      "speakerId": "lorraine",
      "trigger": "confession",
      "text": "Daddy promised Old Goldie to me on the Fourth of July, 1979, on that very dock, and June knows it. So yes. I took the spare key out of her sewing tin, I took the lure, and it is tucked in the sleeve of her birthday sweater. I was going to give it back, dear. Eventually."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_end_correct",
      "speakerId": "narrator",
      "trigger": "ending_correct",
      "text": "A tuft of cranberry mohair on the hinge. Lorraine's fingerprints on the fresh tape in the sewing tin. And a promise from 1979 that only one sister remembers. Old Goldie turns up in the sleeve of a half-knit birthday sweater, and by midnight June and Lorraine have agreed to share custody: six months on each mantel, and Marv still can't have it."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_end_partial",
      "speakerId": "narrator",
      "trigger": "ending_partial",
      "text": "You might be onto something. But Grandma June wants proof she can wave across the table, and you haven't laid any down yet."
    },
    {
      "id": "a_end_wrong",
      "speakerId": "narrator",
      "trigger": "ending_wrong",
      "text": "The accused turns three shades of red, Grandma June sets down her coffee very slowly, and the real thief helps themselves to another slice of cake."
    }
  ]
